<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!-- Generated by scripts/build-group-policy.py; don't edit by hand. -->
<policyDefinitionResources xmlns:xsd="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema" xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance" revision="1.0" schemaVersion="1.0" xmlns="http://www.microsoft.com/GroupPolicy/PolicyDefinitions">
  <displayName>AI Account Guard</displayName>
  <description>Settings for the AI Account Guard browser extension in Google Chrome and Microsoft Edge.</description>
  <resources>
    <stringTable>
      <string id="SUPPORTED_AAG">AI Account Guard 0.1.5 or later</string>
      <string id="Cat_AIAccountGuard">AI Account Guard</string>
      <string id="Mode_enforce">Work accounts only</string>
      <string id="Mode_block">Block entirely</string>
      <string id="Mode_off">Not managed</string>
      <string id="Scope_ai_sites">Only sign-ins started from an AI service</string>
      <string id="Scope_all_browsing">All Google and Microsoft sign-ins in the browser</string>
      <string id="Cat_Chrome">Google Chrome</string>
      <string id="Cat_Chrome_Services">AI services</string>
      <string id="Chrome_AllowedDomains">Allowed email domains</string>
      <string id="Chrome_AllowedDomains_Explain">Email domains whose accounts may sign in to the AI services AI Account Guard covers, one per line, for example example.org. Start a domain with *. to also allow its subdomains, for example *.example.org.

Configure this setting whenever you use AI Account Guard. If it is missing or empty, every sign-in to a covered AI service is blocked.

If none of the AI Account Guard settings reach the browser, an extension that you force-install blocks every AI sign-in and tells users IT is still setting it up.

This setting applies to Google Chrome. Use the same setting under Microsoft Edge for that browser.</string>
      <string id="Chrome_OrganizationName">Organization name</string>
      <string id="Chrome_OrganizationName_Explain">Your organization's name, shown to users on block messages.

This setting applies to Google Chrome. Use the same setting under Microsoft Edge for that browser.</string>
      <string id="Chrome_SsoPortalUrl">Company sign-in portal</string>
      <string id="Chrome_SsoPortalUrl_Explain">The http or https address of your company sign-in portal, for example https://myapps.microsoft.com. Block messages then show a button that opens it, so users can sign in with their work account.

This setting applies to Google Chrome. Use the same setting under Microsoft Edge for that browser.</string>
      <string id="Chrome_SupportMessage">Support message</string>
      <string id="Chrome_SupportMessage_Explain">Extra text shown on block messages, such as how to contact IT.

This setting applies to Google Chrome. Use the same setting under Microsoft Edge for that browser.</string>
      <string id="Chrome_AllowGoogleSignIn">Allow Sign in with Google for work accounts</string>
      <string id="Chrome_AllowGoogleSignIn_Explain">If you enable or don't configure this setting, users can use "Sign in with Google" on covered AI services with a Google Workspace account in your allowed email domains. AI Account Guard tells Google which domains are allowed, and Google refuses personal Google accounts.

If you disable this setting, "Sign in with Google" is blocked on covered AI services.

This setting applies to Google Chrome. Use the same setting under Microsoft Edge for that browser.</string>
      <string id="Chrome_AllowMicrosoftSignIn">Allow Sign in with Microsoft for your tenant</string>
      <string id="Chrome_AllowMicrosoftSignIn_Explain">If you enable this setting, users can use "Sign in with Microsoft" on covered AI services with an account in your Microsoft Entra tenant, using Microsoft tenant restrictions. Also configure "Microsoft tenant ID". Personal Microsoft accounts are always blocked.

If you disable or don't configure this setting, "Sign in with Microsoft" is blocked on covered AI services.

This setting applies to Google Chrome. Use the same setting under Microsoft Edge for that browser.</string>
      <string id="Chrome_MicrosoftTenantId">Microsoft tenant ID</string>
      <string id="Chrome_MicrosoftTenantId_Explain">Your Microsoft Entra directory (tenant) ID, for example 00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000000. It is on the Overview page of the Microsoft Entra admin center. Required when "Allow Sign in with Microsoft for your tenant" is enabled.

This setting applies to Google Chrome. Use the same setting under Microsoft Edge for that browser.</string>
      <string id="Chrome_TenantRestrictionScope">Where Google and Microsoft account restrictions apply</string>
      <string id="Chrome_TenantRestrictionScope_Explain">Sets where the Google and Microsoft account restrictions from the settings above apply.

Only sign-ins started from an AI service: the restrictions apply only when a user signs in to a covered AI service with Google or Microsoft.
All Google and Microsoft sign-ins in the browser: the restrictions apply to every Google and Microsoft sign-in, so personal Google and Microsoft accounts can't sign in anywhere in the browser.

If you disable or don't configure this setting, the restrictions apply only to sign-ins started from an AI service.

This setting applies to Google Chrome. Use the same setting under Microsoft Edge for that browser.</string>
      <string id="Chrome_SignOutOnInstall">Sign users out of AI services on install</string>
      <string id="Chrome_SignOutOnInstall_Explain">If you enable this setting, AI Account Guard clears the cookies and site data of covered AI services when it is first installed, so sessions that were already signed in end and users sign in again with their work account. Services that share Google's or Microsoft's own sign-in (Gemini and Copilot) are skipped, so users aren't signed out of Google or Microsoft altogether.

If you disable or don't configure this setting, existing sessions are not cleared.

This setting applies to Google Chrome. Use the same setting under Microsoft Edge for that browser.</string>
      <string id="Chrome_ProviderModes_chatgpt">ChatGPT</string>
      <string id="Chrome_ProviderModes_chatgpt_Explain">Sets how AI Account Guard handles ChatGPT.

Work accounts only: users can use ChatGPT only when signed in with an account in your allowed email domains.
Block entirely: ChatGPT is blocked.
Not managed: AI Account Guard leaves ChatGPT alone.

If you disable or don't configure this setting, ChatGPT uses its default: Work accounts only.

This setting applies to Google Chrome. Use the same setting under Microsoft Edge for that browser.</string>
      <string id="Chrome_ProviderModes_claude">Claude</string>
      <string id="Chrome_ProviderModes_claude_Explain">Sets how AI Account Guard handles Claude.

Work accounts only: users can use Claude only when signed in with an account in your allowed email domains.
Block entirely: Claude is blocked.
Not managed: AI Account Guard leaves Claude alone.

If you disable or don't configure this setting, Claude uses its default: Work accounts only.

This setting applies to Google Chrome. Use the same setting under Microsoft Edge for that browser.</string>
      <string id="Chrome_ProviderModes_gemini">Gemini</string>
      <string id="Chrome_ProviderModes_gemini_Explain">Sets how AI Account Guard handles Gemini.

Work accounts only: users can use Gemini only when signed in with an account in your allowed email domains.
Block entirely: Gemini is blocked.
Not managed: AI Account Guard leaves Gemini alone.

If you disable or don't configure this setting, Gemini uses its default: Work accounts only.

This setting applies to Google Chrome. Use the same setting under Microsoft Edge for that browser.</string>
      <string id="Chrome_ProviderModes_copilot">Microsoft Copilot (consumer)</string>
      <string id="Chrome_ProviderModes_copilot_Explain">Sets how AI Account Guard handles Microsoft Copilot (consumer).

Work accounts only: users can use Microsoft Copilot (consumer) only when signed in with an account in your allowed email domains.
Block entirely: Microsoft Copilot (consumer) is blocked.
Not managed: AI Account Guard leaves Microsoft Copilot (consumer) alone.

If you disable or don't configure this setting, Microsoft Copilot (consumer) uses its default: Work accounts only.

This setting applies to Google Chrome. Use the same setting under Microsoft Edge for that browser.</string>
      <string id="Chrome_ProviderModes_perplexity">Perplexity</string>
      <string id="Chrome_ProviderModes_perplexity_Explain">Sets how AI Account Guard handles Perplexity.

Work accounts only: users can use Perplexity only when signed in with an account in your allowed email domains.
Block entirely: Perplexity is blocked.
Not managed: AI Account Guard leaves Perplexity alone.

If you disable or don't configure this setting, Perplexity uses its default: Work accounts only.

This setting applies to Google Chrome. Use the same setting under Microsoft Edge for that browser.</string>
      <string id="Chrome_ProviderModes_deepseek">DeepSeek</string>
      <string id="Chrome_ProviderModes_deepseek_Explain">Sets how AI Account Guard handles DeepSeek.

Work accounts only: users can use DeepSeek only when signed in with an account in your allowed email domains.
Block entirely: DeepSeek is blocked.
Not managed: AI Account Guard leaves DeepSeek alone.

If you disable or don't configure this setting, DeepSeek uses its default: Work accounts only.

This setting applies to Google Chrome. Use the same setting under Microsoft Edge for that browser.</string>
